<p class="sst-lead">A "preshape" is the raw image of a curve; a <strong>shape</strong> is what's left after we
quotient out the nuisances we don't care about.</p>

<p>{% raw %}\[ [\,\mathcal{K}(\bm{c})\,] \in \mathcal{C}_d \big/ \big(O(d)\times
\cancelto{\{\,\textcolor{#6ea262}{\gamma}\,\}}{\mathrm{Diff}(\mathcal{I})}\big) \]{% endraw %}</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>That struck-through \(\mathrm{Diff}(\mathcal{I})\) is the <strong>finite</strong> caveat. The
  reparametrization group is infinite-dimensional in general &mdash; but we never instantiate all of it. Once a
  shape is <strong>collocated at fixed quadrature nodes</strong>, its parametrization collapses to a single
  representative {% raw %}\(\{\textcolor{#6ea262}{\gamma}\}\){% endraw %}: discretization <em>is</em> a choice of reparametrization.</p>
</div>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>We need finite representations of shape that are</p>
  <ul>
    <li>rigid-invariant <em>representers</em> (beyond area and perimeter),</li>
    <li>consistent under <em>discretization</em> (a fixed reparametrization),</li>
    <li><em>standardized</em> against generalized scale (not just dilation),</li>
    <li>extensible to <em>tens of thousands</em> of shapes.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p class="sst-claim">The next few scenes build exactly such a representer &mdash; and the punchline is that it
  separates <strong>undulation</strong> from <strong>scale</strong>.</p>
</div>
